### try-catch-finally
🔹 try block
- Contains risky code
- Exception may occur here
🔹 catch block
- Handles the exception
- Prevents program crash
- Can have multiple catch blocks
🔹 finally block
- Always executes (exception ho ya na ho)
- Used for resource cleanup (closing file, DB, etc.)
Syntax
try {
    // risky code
} catch (ExceptionType e) {
    // exception handling
} finally {
    // cleanup code
}

Key Points to Remember
. Use try for risky code - This is where errors might happen.
. Use catch to handle specific exceptions - Always catch the most accurate exception, not the general Exception .
. Use finally to clean up - It always runs, whether or not there's an error.
. Avoid return in finally - It can override returns from try or catch and cause confusion.
. Don't leave catch blocks empty - Always give a message or fix the problem.
. Use finally for important tasks like closing files, stopping connections, or cleaning up.

Case 1:- if there is a no exception
public class Main 
{
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		try {
			System.out.println("try");
		} catch (Exception e) {
			System.out.println("catch");
		}
		finally {
			System.out.println("finally");
		}
	}
}
Output:- 
try
finally

case2:- if an exception rise and handled
public class Main1 {
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		try {
			System.out.println("try");
			System.out.println(10/0);
		} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
			System.out.println("catch");
		}
		finally {
			System.out.println("finally");
		}
	}
}
Output:- 
try
catch
finally

Case3:- 
if exception raised but not handled
public class Main2 {
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		try {
			System.out.println("try");
			System.out.println(10/0);
		} catch(NullPointerException e) {
			System.out.println("catch");
		} finally {
			System.out.println("finally");
		}
	}
}
